  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is ghrelin?",
      "a": "Ghrelin is a hormone made mainly by the stomach when it is empty. It travels in the blood to the brain and makes you feel hungry. Its level drops after you eat.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about the empty stomach",
        "What is the hunger hormone?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What tells you that you are full?",
      "a": "Stretch sensors in the stomach send nerve signals, and the intestines release fullness hormones when food arrives. These reach the brain, which switches off hunger.",
      "asks": [
        "How do you know when you have eaten enough?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is leptin?",
      "a": "Leptin is a hormone released by fat tissue. The more energy is stored as fat, the more leptin there is, and it tells the brain about the body's long-term energy stores.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about the fat stores"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the hypothalamus do in hunger?",
      "a": "The hypothalamus is a small area at the base of the brain that collects hunger and fullness signals from the body and turns them into feelings of hunger or satisfaction.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about the hypothalamus"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does your stomach rumble when you're hungry?",
      "a": "When the stomach and intestines are empty, waves of muscle squeezing sweep through them to clear leftovers. With little food inside, the gas and fluid being pushed along make loud gurgles.",
      "asks": [
        "What is tummy rumbling?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Ghrelin, discovered in 1999, is made mainly in the stomach and stimulates hunger.",
    "Leptin is released by fat tissue and signals long-term energy stores to the brain."
  ],
